WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.458
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.458 --> 00:00:04.440
I help some medical
professionals improve

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.300
their video upload speeds.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.640
Will their performance get
a clean bill of health?

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.600
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.410
A few weeks ago, I met
with the Medic 7 research

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.470
group, who were seeing a
consistent problem with the GCS

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.760
upload speeds they had.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.870
Their clients would make
a video of a genetic test

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.470
or a chemical reaction
and then spend

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.790
a number of hours waiting
for the four-gigabyte file

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:27.720
to upload.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.511
When you're moving at
the speed of science,

00:00:29.511 --> 00:00:31.380
those type of delays are costly.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.090
To get a sense of what
upload speeds look

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.220
like with very large
files, I pushed

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.250
a bunch of 100-meg,
200-meg, and 500-meg files

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.540
to GCS using the Python API
and charted their performance.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.150
While still really
fast, we see that there

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.250
appears to be a slight
upper limit with respect

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.257
to upload speeds for
a single large file.

00:00:50.257 --> 00:00:52.590
What's interesting about the
problem is that the Medic 7

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:56.520
group had only ever been using
the Python API for uploads,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.550
because if you use the
gsutil for the same option,

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.470
you get this handy
warning any time

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.560
you try to upload a large
file, which politely suggests

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.710
that you should be using
composite uploads for the best

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:08.720
performance.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.100
See, with composite
uploads, gsutil

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.920
can split a large file
into smaller pieces, which

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.930
are uploaded in parallel
and then composed

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.420
back together in
your GCS bucket while

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.906
removing all the temporary
components properly.

00:01:20.906 --> 00:01:22.530
For Medic 7, the
performance difference

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.800
between the blob and composite
uploads is immediately visible.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.050
Running the same test showed
almost a 2x improvement

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.730
in upload times.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.340
For more information on using
composite parallel uploads

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.730
and how to work with the crcmod
requirement for the clients,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.380
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.620
And for more great tips
on optimizing your cloud,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.995
make sure to check out the
rest of the "Cloud Performance

00:01:42.995 --> 00:01:43.650
Atlas" content.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.233
Because, when it
comes to performance,

00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:47.420
every millisecond counts.

